  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The country became a member of the ILO in 2012. Its total ratification record comprises of seven fundamental ILO Conventions on 29 April 2012. Convention 87 remains unratified. The South Sudan Labour Bill was drafted with the help of ILO experts in 2011 and enacted into the Labour Law in 2017. Implementation rules and regulations remain largely absent. 
Its governance institutions as mandated by the labour act, where they exist, are of recent date and being developed to increase their efficiencies and effectiveness. Others are awaiting their establishment. The challenge now is to create all institutions to implement the law and awareness among the populace and to fully implement the law. 
As a first step, a Tripartite Labour Advisory Council was established in 2021 with technical assistance of the ILO to facilitate the implementation of the labour law across the country.
The support under SSD802 will aim at assisting the government, to ratify C. 144 on tripartite consultations (ILS), in line with the functioning and mandate of the established tripartite Labour Advisory Council (LAC). A formal commitment was expressed by the Government to this effect. Currently, the Ministry of Justice is reviewing the C. 144 ratification process. 
Further support remains required under the CPO on any ILO Constitutional obligation of reporting on the ratified Conventions. In this regard, support will continue to build the tripartite capacity on ILS reporting.
BRMM and JMLP migration projects maintain ILS related assistance related to migration conventions, C. 189 on domestic work and C. 190 on violence and harassment. For the latter, the government has expressed interest in ratification, but no concrete steps are being made to pursue it. The technical assistance did result in draft regulation on C. 181 related to private employment agencies, and this calls for support to enhance capacity of the constituents to move ahead on such initiatives in an effective and joint manner.</narrative>
  </description>
